<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Design Document</title></head><body><div style="font-family: Segoe UI, Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; color: #ffffff; background-color: #1a1a2e; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px;"><h1 style="color: #60a5fa; border-bottom: 3px solid #60a5fa; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-bottom: 20px; font-size: 28px;">Design Document</h1><p style="color: #cbd5e1; font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 20px; font-size: 14px;">Generated by Mistral Small on 9/16/2026, 2:59:50 PM | Session: 40ffc33c...</p><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">1. Architecture Overview</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Architecture Overview**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1. Introduction**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">This section outlines the high-level architecture design for the Patient Health Care System (PHCS), a system designed to automate onboarding and collaboration services with the primary objective of enhancing patient care. The system focuses on core functions, streamlining healthcare partner integration, improving data integrity, and increasing operational efficiency through automation.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**2. System Architecture and Design Philosophy**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The PHCS architecture follows a microservices-based approach, allowing for independent development, deployment, and scaling of services. This design philosophy promotes modularity, flexibility, and ease of maintenance. Each microservice is designed to perform a specific function, reducing the complexity of the overall system.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**3. Key Architectural Decisions and Rationale**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">*Microservices Architecture*: The microservices architecture was chosen for its ability to accommodate the system's complex requirements while promoting scalability, fault tolerance, and ease of maintenance.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">*API Gateway*: An API Gateway will serve as the main entry point for all external and internal requests. It will manage and route requests to the appropriate microservices, ensuring a consistent and secure interface for partners and internal systems.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">*Service Mesh*: A service mesh will be used for service-to-service communication, providing features such as load balancing, traffic routing, and fault tolerance.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">*Data Management*: A combination of relational databases and NoSQL databases will be used to store patient, program, and consent-related data. Relational databases will be used for structured data, while NoSQL databases will handle unstructured data and provide high scalability.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**4. Major Components and Their Relationships**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**API Gateway**: The first point of contact for all requests, it routes requests to the appropriate microservices.</li></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">2. Component Design</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Component Design**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1. Patient Registration and Enrollment Management System (PREMS)**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The Patient Registration and Enrollment Management System (PREMS) is the core component responsible for managing patient registration, enrollment, and related processes. It serves as the central hub for patient data, ensuring data consistency and integrity across the system.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.1 Responsibilities and Boundaries**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">PREMS is responsible for handling the creation, update, and deletion of patient records, managing patient enrollments in various programs, and capturing, validating, and tracking patient consents. It interacts primarily with internal healthcare systems, external clinical partners, and the Consent Management System (CMS). PREMS does not handle direct patient care, diagnostic services, or pharmaceutical manufacturing.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.2 Inter-Component Communication**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">PREMS communicates with internal healthcare systems using HL7 (Health Level Seven) standards for data exchange. For external partner integration, RESTful APIs will be utilized. Real-time and scheduled data submissions are processed through a message broker (e.g., RabbitMQ or Apache Kafka).</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.3 Lifecycle and Deployment Considerations**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">PREMS will be deployed as a microservice within a containerized environment (e.g., Docker) for easier scalability and maintenance. It will be designed to handle high volumes of data, ensuring minimal latency during patient registration and enrollment processes.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.4 Component Interaction Diagram**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">[Insert diagram showing PREMS interaction with internal healthcare systems, CMS, and message broker]</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.5 Configuration and Customization**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">PREMS will provide configurable workflows and mappings to enable rapid onboarding of new partners. It will also support customizable user roles and permissions to ensure</p></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">3. Data Models</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Data Models Section**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">In this section, we will define the primary data entities, their relationships, data schemas, data consistency and integrity constraints, data lifecycle, indexing, query optimization strategies, and provide ER diagrams or data relationship descriptions for the Patient Health Care System.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Patient Entity**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The Patient entity is the central data object in our system, which stores patient-related information.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Attributes:**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">PatientID (Primary Key, Auto-incrementing integer)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">FirstName (Varchar, not null)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">LastName (Varchar, not null)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">DateOfBirth (Date, not null)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Gender (Enum: Male, Female, Other)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Address (JSON object, optional)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">ContactDetails (JSON object, optional)</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Relationships:**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">One-to-Many with Enrollment entity (Patient can have multiple enrollments)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">One-to-Many with Consent entity (Patient can have multiple consents)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Enrollment Entity**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The Enrollment entity represents a patient's enrollment in a specific program or service.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Attributes:**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">EnrollmentID (Primary Key, Auto-incrementing integer)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">PatientID (Foreign Key, references Patient.PatientID)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">EnrollmentDate (Date, not null)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">ProgramName (Varchar, not null)</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Relationships:**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">One-to-Many with Consent entity (Enrollment can have multiple consents)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Consent Entity**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The Consent entity stores the consent information provided by the patient for each enrollment.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Attributes:**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">ConsentID (Primary Key, Auto-incrementing integer)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Enrollment</li></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">4. Interface Definitions</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Interface Definitions**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1. Patient Registration and Enrollment Management API**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.1 Endpoint**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">POST /api/v1/patients</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">PUT /api/v1/patients/{patientId}</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">GET /api/v1/patients/{patientId}</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">DELETE /api/v1/patients/{patientId}</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.2 Request/Response**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.2.1 Request**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">`Content-Type: application/json`</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For POST and PUT requests:</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```json</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">{</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"firstName": "John",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"lastName": "Doe",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"dateOfBirth": "1990-01-01",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"gender": "M",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"email": "john.doe@example.com",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"phoneNumber": "1234567890",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"enrollmentId": "ENR-12345",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"partnerId": "PART-67890"</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">}</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For GET requests, an optional `query` parameter can be used to filter results by `patientId`, `enrollmentId`, or `partnerId`.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.2.2 Response**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">`Content-Type: application/json`</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```json</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">{</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"patientId": "PAT-12345",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"firstName": "John",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"lastName": "Doe",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"dateOfBirth": "1990-01-01",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"gender": "M",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"email": "john.doe@example.com",</p></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">5. API Specifications</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**API Specifications**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1. Patient Registration and Enrollment Management**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1.1 Endpoints**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">POST `/api/v1/patients`: Create a new patient profile</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Request:</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```json</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">{</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"first_name": "John",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"last_name": "Doe",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"date_of_birth": "YYYY-MM-DD",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"email": "johndoe@example.com",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"phone_number": "1234567890"</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">}</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Response (201 Created):</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```json</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">{</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"id": "patient_123",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"status": "active",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"first_name": "John",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"last_name": "Doe",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"date_of_birth": "YYYY-MM-DD",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"email": "johndoe@example.com",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"phone_number": "1234567890"</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">}</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">GET `/api/v1/patients/{patient_id}`: Retrieve patient details by ID</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Request:</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">GET /api/v1/patients/patient_123</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Response (200 OK):</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">```json</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">{</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"id": "patient_123",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"status": "active",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"first_name": "John",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"last_name": "Doe",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"date_of_birth": "YYYY-MM-DD",</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">"email": "johndoe@example.</p></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">6. Security Architecture</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Security Architecture for Patient Health Care System**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The Patient Health Care System (PHCS) is designed to automate onboarding and collaboration processes, streamline healthcare partner integration, enhance data integrity, security, and compliance, and increase operational efficiency. This section outlines the security architecture to address all threat vectors, ensuring the system is secure, compliant, and protects sensitive patient data.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Authentication and Authorization**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To maintain secure access to the PHCS, we will implement an OAuth 2.0-based authentication mechanism. This standardized protocol allows for secure authorization in a simple and flexible manner.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For authentication tokens, JSON Web Tokens (JWT) will be used. JWT provides a compact and self-contained way to transmit information between parties.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) will be used to manage user permissions within the system. This approach ensures that users only have access to the resources they are authorized to handle.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**User Registration:**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">New users will be required to provide their personal information, including email, password, and role.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Upon successful registration, users will receive a verification email.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Upon confirmation, users will receive a JWT token granting them access to the system.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Encryption Requirements**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To secure data in transit, Transport Layer Security (TLS) will be used. TLS is a cryptographic protocol that provides secure and encrypted communication over the internet.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For at-rest encryption, Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) with a 256-bit key will be used. This ensures that data is encrypted when it is not being actively used or transmitted.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Secrets Management and Credential Handling**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To manage and secure sensitive information such</p></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">7. Performance Considerations</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Performance Considerations Section**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Performance Targets**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To ensure smooth operation and user satisfaction, the following performance targets have been set:</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Latency: Aim for a response time of less than 1 second for 95th percentile of requests in the system, with the median response time being less than 50 ms.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Throughput: The system should be able to handle a minimum of 10,000 concurrent users with a consistent throughput of at least 100 transactions per second.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Concurrency: The system should be able to effectively handle multiple operations running simultaneously without significant degradation in performance.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Caching Strategy and Invalidation**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To reduce the load on the database and improve response times, we will employ an LRU (Least Recently Used) cache strategy. The cache will be implemented at various layers, including the application, web server, and database layers.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Caching will be used for frequently accessed data such as patient profiles, consent forms, and common integration APIs. To ensure data consistency, an effective cache invalidation strategy will be implemented. This will involve invalidating the cache when data changes occur, either through updates, deletions, or insertions.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Database Query Optimization and Indexing**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To ensure efficient data retrieval, queries will be optimized to minimize the number of disk accesses and CPU cycles. This includes using SQL best practices, such as indexing frequently queried columns, avoiding SELECT * statements, and minimizing the use of subqueries.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Indexes will be created on all foreign keys, frequently searched columns, and columns used in JOIN operations. Regular database health checks will be conducted to identify and address any performance issues.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Asynchronous Processing for</li></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">8. Error Handling Strategy</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Error Handling Strategy**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">In the context of the Patient Health Care System, a robust and comprehensive error handling strategy is essential to maintain system resilience, ensure data integrity, and provide a seamless user experience. This strategy is designed to handle errors and exceptions that may occur during the execution of the in-scope functions, specifically patient registration, enrollment management, consent capture/validation, and partner integration.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Error Classification System**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The error handling strategy will classify errors into two categories: retriable and non-retriable. Retriable errors are temporary issues that can be resolved by retrying the operation, while non-retriable errors are permanent or critical issues that require manual intervention or system recovery.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Retry Logic with Exponential Backoff**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For retriable errors, the system will implement retry logic with exponential backoff. This means that if an error occurs, the system will attempt to retry the operation, with each subsequent attempt progressively delaying the retry to reduce the load on the server and minimize the risk of throttling. This approach helps to recover from temporary network issues, server overloads, or other transient errors.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Circuit Breaker Pattern**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To prevent cascading failures, the system will employ the circuit breaker pattern. When a certain number of failures are detected within a specific timeframe, the circuit breaker trips, and further requests for the affected service are redirected to a fallback mechanism. This approach allows the system to isolate failures and maintain overall system stability.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Error Logging and Monitoring**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The system will log all errors and exceptions, including the error type, error message, timestamp, and contextual information such as the affected service, request data, and response data. This detailed logging will enable the development team to</p></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">9. Logging and Monitoring</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Logging and Monitoring Strategy**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1. Logging Levels and Structured Logging Format**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To ensure comprehensive visibility and traceability, we will employ a structured logging format that includes the following elements:</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Timestamp: ISO 8601 formatted date and time for precise logging.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Log Level: Utilizing SEV(Severity) levels (Emergency, Alert, Critical, Error, Warning, Notice, Info, Debug) to differentiate the urgency and importance of each log entry.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Thread ID: Unique identifier for each thread executing in the system, facilitating debugging and understanding of concurrent processes.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Request ID: A unique identifier associated with each user request, enabling correlation of log entries across multiple services and system components.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Component: Name of the service or system component that generated the log entry, providing context for troubleshooting.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Message: Detailed description of the event or issue that led to the log entry, along with any relevant additional information.</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**2. Metrics Collection**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Metrics will be collected to monitor system performance and health, with the following key performance indicators (KPIs) being tracked:</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">CPU Utilization: Monitoring the average CPU usage across all servers to ensure optimal performance and scalability.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Memory Usage: Tracking the memory consumption of each service to identify potential memory leak issues and ensure efficient resource usage.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Latency: Measuring the response time of each service to identify performance bottlenecks and ensure a responsive user experience.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Error Rates: Calculating the percentage of failed requests to identify trends and potential issues impacting system reliability.</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**3. Alerting Strategy and Escalation Procedures**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">An alerting system will be implemented to notify relevant personnel of critical issues</p></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">10. Deployment Architecture</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Title: Deployment Architecture for Patient Health Care System</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Deployment Topology</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Our Patient Health Care System (PHCS) will be deployed across multiple AWS regions for high availability and fault tolerance. Each region will consist of multiple Availability Zones (AZs), ensuring that services are distributed across physically separate locations within the region. This distribution will help minimize the impact of any potential service disruptions.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Within each AZ, we will deploy a set of Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs) for load balancing and scalability. The ASGs will consist of application servers running on Amazon EC2 instances (t3.large or larger) to handle the varying workload demands.</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For managing stateful services like databases, we will use Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS) with AWS Fargate, ensuring efficient scaling and resource utilization. ECS services will be spread across multiple AZs within a region to improve availability.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Infrastructure-as-Code Approach</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To manage the infrastructure components, we will use Terraform for its ability to define, provision, and manage cloud resources. Terraform will be used to create and update the AWS resources, ensuring consistency and automation in the infrastructure provisioning process.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Database Migration Strategy</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For database migration, we will employ a phased approach. Initially, we will configure a read replica of the source database in the target AWS region. Once the replica is fully synchronized, we will perform a zero-downtime migration to the new database instance using AWS Database Migration Service.</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Zero-Downtime Deployment and Rollback Procedures</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">For deploying application updates, we will use AWS CodePipeline, CodeBuild, and CodeDeploy. CodePipeline will orchestrate the continuous delivery process, while Code</p></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">11. Development Workflow</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Development Workflow**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Local Development Environment Setup**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">To facilitate a smooth development process, it is crucial to set up a consistent and well-configured local development environment. Each developer should install the following tools:</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Operating System: Ubuntu 20.04 LTS or Windows 10 (for WSL)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">IDE: IntelliJ IDEA or Eclipse with the required plugins</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Version Control System: Git (version 2.30.x or later)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Programming Language: Java 11 (OpenJDK)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Database: PostgreSQL 12 or later</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Build Tool: Apache Maven 3.6.x or later</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Containerization: Docker 19.x or later</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Code Organization and Module Structure**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The project will be divided into well-defined modules, each focusing on specific functionalities:</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Core (containing the core functionality, such as Patient, Enrollment, Consent, Integration)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Services (including services like Data Encryption, Error Handling, and Real-Time Data Processing)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Infrastructure (handling database connections, APIs, and Docker configuration)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Utilities (containing utility classes and helpers)</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Git Workflow**</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">The Git workflow consists of the following branches:</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">`master`: Production-ready code and the latest stable version of the application</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">`develop`: The main development branch, where new features and bug fixes are integrated</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">`feature/{name}`: Branches for new features or enhancements</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">`bugfix/{name}`: Branches for fixing bugs or issues</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Code Review and</li></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">12. Testing Strategy</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Testing Strategy for Patient Health Care System**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**1. Test Pyramid**</p><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">Our testing strategy will be based on a well-balanced test pyramid to ensure comprehensive coverage, maintainability, and efficiency. The test pyramid will consist of the following levels:</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Unit Tests (80%)**: These tests will focus on individual modules, functions, and methods ensuring they work correctly in isolation. We will aim to cover 80% of our codebase with unit tests, ensuring a strong foundation for the rest of the testing pyramid.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Integration Tests (15%)**: These tests will validate the interactions between different modules and services within the system. Integration tests will help us catch issues related to dependencies, configurations, and system interfaces.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**End-to-End Tests (5%)**: These tests will simulate real-world user scenarios, ensuring the entire system works as expected in various environments and under different conditions. End-to-end tests will help us verify that the system meets user requirements and provides an acceptable user experience.</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**2. Testing Tools and Frameworks**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Unit Testing**: We will use JUnit for Java-based unit tests and Mocha or Jest for JavaScript-based unit tests. These tools provide a simple, easy-to-use, and flexible framework for writing and running tests.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Integration Testing and End-to-End Testing**: For integration testing and end-to-end testing, we will leverage Selenium WebDriver for browser-based testing, and TestContainers for container-based testing. These tools will enable us to test the system across various configurations and environments.</li><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**3. Test Data Strategy and Fixtures**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Test Data Strategy**: We will employ a combination of real, synthetic, and generated test</li></div><div style="margin-bottom: 32px; border-left: 3px solid #f97316; padding-left: 16px;"><h2 style="color: #f97316; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 18px;">13. Documentation Guidelines</h2><p style="margin: 10px 0; line-height: 1.8; text-align: justify; color: #e2e8f0;">**Documentation Guidelines for Patient Health Care System — Functional Specification**</p><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Document Structure and Organization**</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">The document should be well-structured, easy to navigate, and logically organized.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Sections should be numbered and titled for clarity, with each section representing a distinct topic or functionality.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Subsections can be used to break down larger topics into manageable parts.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Cross-referencing should be used to link related sections, aiding readers in finding relevant information.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**API Documentation Standards**</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">All APIs should be thoroughly documented, including endpoints, methods, input parameters, output formats, and error responses.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Documentation should adhere to industry standards such as Swagger, OpenAPI, or RAML for consistent format and ease of use.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Include authentication and authorization requirements, as well as any rate limitations or quota systems.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Provide examples of API usage, including sample requests and responses, to aid developers in their implementation.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Runbook and Troubleshooting Guide Requirements**</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">A runbook should be created for each critical system process, detailing the steps to execute the process, troubleshoot issues, and recover from failures.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Troubleshooting guides should provide step-by-step instructions for resolving common issues, as well as guidance for escalating more complex problems.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Guides should be written in a clear, concise, and actionable manner, with diagrams and screenshots used to illustrate key concepts.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">**Architecture Decision Record (ADR) Process**</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">An ADR process should be established to document important technical decisions made during the project.</li><li style="margin: 6px 0; line-height: 1.6; margin-left: 20px; color: #f1f5f9;">Each ADR should include a clear problem statement, proposed</li></div></div></body></html>